    "breadcrumb": "Approach \u003e Theoretical Grounding",
    "description": "Working draft for faculty review. This page describes the general research basis for designing course materials with built-in flexibility rather than retrofitted accommodations.\nWhat Universal Design for Learning is Universal Design for Learning (UDL) applies an idea from architecture — design a building’s entrances and spaces to work for the widest range of bodies from the start, rather than building for one “standard” body and bolting on a wheelchair ramp later — to instructional design. CAST’s foundational articulation frames the target of instruction as the “expert learner,” and its central premise is that learner variability is the norm, not the exception: a course designed for one imagined average student will systematically underserve everyone who doesn’t match that profile, whereas a course built with multiple ways in from the outset serves a much wider range of learners without requiring an individual accommodation request for each one (Rose \u0026 Meyer, 2002). The current operational version of the framework is maintained and periodically revised by CAST (CAST, 2024).",
    "modified": "2026-09-28T03:08:36+00:00",
